{"step_index":1452,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-20T00:35:48Z","content":"Since this is an industrial quality intelligence platform, you have a few ways to deploy it depending on your security and scale requirements. Factory-floor data (like production telemetry and defect images) is usually sensitive, so I've created a **production-ready `Dockerfile`** in your project folder to give you the most flexibility.\n\nHere are the 3 best ways to deploy this platform, ranging from easiest (Demo) to most secure (On-Premise/Factory Floor):\n\n### 1. The Industrial / Secure Way (On-Premise via Docker)\nSince you'll likely want to run this locally on the factory network so telemetry data never leaves the facility, deploying via Docker is the industry standard. \n\nI just added a `Dockerfile` to your project that handles all the heavy lifting (including the C++ graphics libraries required by YOLO's OpenCV).\n\n**How to run it:**\n1. Install Docker on your host server.\n2. Open PowerShell or your terminal in the project folder and build the container:\n   ```bash\n   docker build -t neurax-app .\n   ```\n3. Run the container, mapping it to port 80:\n   ```bash\n   docker run -d -p 80:8501 --name neurax-platform neurax-app\n   ```\n*Your platform will now be accessible to anyone on the factory network by visiting the server's local IP address (e.g., `http://192.168.1.100`).*\n\n### 2. The Cloud Enterprise Way (Google Cloud Run / AWS / Azure)\nIf you want executives to access this globally without VPNs, you can push that exact same Docker container to a serverless cloud provider. \n* **Google Cloud Run:** This is highly recommended because it scales to zero (costs nothing when no one is looking at the dashboard) but handles heavy AI workloads seamlessly.\n* **Steps:**\n  1. Install the Google Cloud CLI (`gcloud`).\n  2. Run `gcloud run deploy neurax-platform --source . --port 8501 --memory 4Gi`\n  3. It will return a secure `https://...` URL you can share with your team. \n\n### 3. The Quickest Demo Way (Streamlit Community Cloud)\nIf you just want to show this off to a client or stakeholder immediately for free, you can use Streamlit's official cloud host.\n1. Push your project code to a public or private **GitHub** repository.\n   *(Note: Because YOLOv8 `.pt` weights are large, you'll need to use Git LFS [Large File Storage] when pushing those files).*\n2. Go to [share.streamlit.io](https://share.streamlit.io/).\n3. Connect your GitHub account and point it to `app.py`.\n4. Streamlit will build and host it automatically on a public URL.\n\n**A quick tip before you deploy:**\nMake sure you replace the default `.env` configuration or use Streamlit Secrets to securely inject your `ENV_SCRAP_COST` and `ENV_DOWNTIME_COST` variables in production! \n\nWhich environment are you aiming for—an internal factory server, a cloud platform, or a quick web demo? I can write the specific deployment script or setup instructions for you!"}
